# CYGNSS historical two-line element sets, retrieved from the Internet Archive's
# snapshots of CelesTrak's General Perturbations service.
#
# Why the Internet Archive: CelesTrak's own historical archive
# (https://celestrak.org/NORAD/archives/) covers 1980 to 2004 and refers any other
# date to a Special Data Request; Space-Track's gp_history class does serve
# arbitrary past epochs but requires a registered account, and there are no
# Space-Track credentials on this machine. CelesTrak's live endpoint serves only
# the newest element set per object. The Wayback Machine's captures of the live
# endpoint are therefore the reachable public record of what the catalog held near
# the telemetry window of 2025-06-06 .. 2025-06-09.
#
# Two captures bracket the 2025-06-06T00:00:00Z common epoch. Both are recorded
# here so the choice between them can be re-measured rather than asserted; see
# build_cygnss_ics.jl, which scores each against the FM1 and FM4 flight telemetry.
#
# Retrieved 2026-09-16.
#
# Block A source:
#   http://web.archive.org/web/20250528012741id_/https://celestrak.org/NORAD/elements/gp.php?GROUP=active&FORMAT=tle
#   capture timestamp 2025-05-28T01:27:41Z, element epochs 2025-05-25 .. 2025-05-27
# Block B source:
#   http://web.archive.org/web/20250612182926id_/https://celestrak.org/NORAD/elements/gp.php?GROUP=weather&FORMAT=tle
#   capture timestamp 2025-06-12T18:29:26Z, element epochs 2025-06-11 .. 2025-06-12
#
# CYGFM06 (NORAD 41889, 2016-078F) appears in neither capture: CelesTrak's SATCAT
# records it decayed on 2024-06-13. See the JSON file's notes.

